WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:06.630
The president and the hairbrained people around
him are increasingly fear mongering around

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:11.070
the stock market, saying if Biden wins in
November.

00:00:11.070 --> 00:00:13.590
Say goodbye to your four or one case.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:18.230
Here's actually a tweet from Donald Trump
yesterday where he said, quote, If you want

00:00:18.230 --> 00:00:23.920
your Forell one KS and stocks, which are getting
close to an all time high, Nasdaq is already

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:27.650
there to disintegrate and disappear.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:33.180
Vote for the radical left, do nothing Democrats
and corrupt Joe Biden.

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:35.090
Massive tax hikes.

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:37.550
They will make you very poor fast.

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:43.350
Now, I already dealt yesterday with how the
overwhelming majority of Joe Biden's tax increases

00:00:43.350 --> 00:00:45.170
go to the top one percent.

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.800
So I don't want to get derailed here.

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:52.629
But remember my segment about how populist
rhetoric can be used to bamboozle people?

00:00:52.629 --> 00:00:54.929
Trump ran with populist rhetoric.

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:59.159
He's for the middle class and he's against
all the oppressive factors that are repressing

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:00.159
the middle class.

00:01:00.159 --> 00:01:05.850
Joe Biden is going to raise taxes on the very
rich on the top one percent and Trump populist.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:09.210
Trump is now campaigning against it and scaring
people about it.

00:01:09.210 --> 00:01:11.900
Doesn't sound very populist to me, does it,
anyway?

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:13.830
That's not what I want to deal with today.

00:01:13.830 --> 00:01:16.120
I want to look at this claim.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:19.600
Biden would be bad for the stock market from
a few different angles.

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:22.780
Now, the first is just a reminder.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:28.730
Again, they are lying in the sense that the
stock market does better under Democratic

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:29.730
presidents.

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:31.620
I will get to that data in a moment.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:35.370
But the other side of this that I think is
always important to repeat when something

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:43.170
like this comes up is that the Trump administration
continues to say or believe or claim or pretend

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.420
that you can measure how well an economy is
doing by looking at the stock market.

00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:47.420
And you can't.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:50.950
So before we even get into the numbers of
Democrats versus Republicans on the stock

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.040
market.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:56.650
Let me remind you, stock market performance
does not tell you how well the economy is

00:01:56.650 --> 00:01:57.650
doing.

00:01:57.650 --> 00:02:01.210
Stocks are disproportionately owned by the
wealthy and by big corporations.

00:02:01.210 --> 00:02:05.890
The more we learn about how do we evaluate
how well or poorly an economy is doing.

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:10.030
We understand that the stock market is a component,
but it is merely one of many components we

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.030
should be looking at.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:14.600
We must look at the employment rate and unemployment
rate.

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:16.290
We have to look at wage growth.

00:02:16.290 --> 00:02:18.950
We have to look at the quality of jobs.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.590
We must look at.

00:02:20.590 --> 00:02:22.269
Inflation and inequality.

00:02:22.269 --> 00:02:25.730
And really, you want to look at quality of
life factors as well.

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:29.090
What is life expectancy, educational factors?

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:30.090
Where is literacy?

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.090
Right.

00:02:31.090 --> 00:02:33.580
There are so many ways to figure out, like,
how is a country doing?

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:35.209
A big picture.

00:02:35.209 --> 00:02:40.549
But let's for a second grant them that there
isn't empirical reality.

00:02:40.549 --> 00:02:44.829
Stock markets either do better under Democratic
presidential administrations or Republican

00:02:44.829 --> 00:02:46.299
presidential administrations.

00:02:46.299 --> 00:02:47.299
We can measure it.

00:02:47.299 --> 00:02:49.049
We can know this.

00:02:49.049 --> 00:02:51.989
There are a whole bunch of different ways
to do it.

00:02:51.989 --> 00:02:53.730
So you've got to make some decisions.

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:55.650
Like I'll give you some examples.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:59.629
Do you would just stock market for performance
for inflation or not?

00:02:59.629 --> 00:03:07.180
When comparing Democratic and Republican performance,
do you calculate returns with dividends reinvested

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:08.180
or not?

00:03:08.180 --> 00:03:09.180
OK.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.599
So there's like little tweaks in terms of
how you calculate it, no matter how you do

00:03:12.599 --> 00:03:13.829
it.

00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:19.609
Every serious analysis finds that stocks do
better under Democratic presidents than they

00:03:19.609 --> 00:03:21.730
do under Republican presidents.

00:03:21.730 --> 00:03:29.519
If you go back to 1929, on average, stocks
make about 10 percent a year during Democratic

00:03:29.519 --> 00:03:34.950
presidencies and about two percent a year
during Republican presidencies.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:40.739
That's one study from Forbes, another study
that looked only at the last forty five years

00:03:40.739 --> 00:03:41.739
or so.

00:03:41.739 --> 00:03:47.120
Found that the stock market return is about
14 percent per year under Democratic presidents,

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.180
10 percent per year under Republican presidents.

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:55.840
Now, the difficulty of these numbers is that
you can have sort of outlier years that can

00:03:55.840 --> 00:03:57.980
skew the averages.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:03.099
But from an empirical, historical perspective,
the stock market does better when there's

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:05.799
a Democrat in the White House instead of a
Republican.

00:04:05.799 --> 00:04:07.209
But that's looking back.

00:04:07.209 --> 00:04:12.079
OK, we then should analyze if we want to do
this thoroughly.

00:04:12.079 --> 00:04:18.630
Would changing from Trump to Biden likely
raise the value of stock prices, lower the

00:04:18.630 --> 00:04:22.320
value or or make no change whatsoever?

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:23.320
Very interesting.

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:33.510
New research from JP Morgan, which finds that
if you look at a Biden replacement of Trump,

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:39.340
it would either be neutral or slightly positive
in terms of stock prices.

00:04:39.340 --> 00:04:45.730
And they also mention that adding a, quote,
diplomatic approach could be good for stocks

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:47.140
in that it would reduce volatility.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:50.370
Now, this is funny because, you know, it's
JP Morgan.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:53.470
They're trying to just make it a just the
facts analysis.

00:04:53.470 --> 00:04:55.730
They don't want to be political.

00:04:55.730 --> 00:05:00.070
Diplomatic approach is a polite term for Biden
will be more normal.

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:01.070
Right.

00:05:01.070 --> 00:05:03.810
Biden will not constantly do crazy stuff.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:08.710
Markets like normal markets like less volatility
at the other component.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:09.830
That's more of an intangible.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:15.630
If we think about the effect of Biden replacing
Trump on the stock market is a Biden presidency

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:17.810
would better deal with Corona virus.

00:05:17.810 --> 00:05:22.210
And because Corona virus is really the major
drag on the economy right now.

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:27.390
There are additional benefits we would expect
to the economy from Joe Biden taking over

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:28.500
for Donald Trump.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:35.130
We interestingly, we saw this under Bush,
Obama, the economy cratered under George W.

00:05:35.130 --> 00:05:36.130
Bush.

00:05:36.130 --> 00:05:39.420
Of course, 9/11 was a part of it, but certainly
going to war with a country that didn't attack

00:05:39.420 --> 00:05:46.670
us, did not help, helped some defense stocks,
but it did not help the economy overall.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:51.560
We saw the mortgage crisis devolve into the
2007 2008 recession.

00:05:51.560 --> 00:05:56.210
And then Barack Obama came in, inaugurated
in January of 2009 and started to clean it

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:57.210
up.

00:05:57.210 --> 00:05:58.660
Could have better cleaned it up for sure.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:05:59.690
Didn't do everything right.

00:05:59.690 --> 00:06:01.610
But that was the way it went.

00:06:01.610 --> 00:06:05.070
We could end up with a very similar dynamic
under Trump.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:09.200
Biden if Donald Trump is actually removed
in November and made a one term president,

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:13.530
not a guarantee, but something that could
happen, we could see a similar dynamic where

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.630
we see a proper economic recovery once again
under a Democratic president.

00:06:18.630 --> 00:06:19.900
It remains to be seen.

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:26.510
But the point is the argument that Biden would
be worse for stocks than Trump, it doesn't

00:06:26.510 --> 00:06:31.150
fit the historical realities of stock markets
under Democrats versus Republicans.

00:06:31.150 --> 00:06:36.330
And it doesn't even pass the sniff test when
you think about replacing kooky, wild, wacky

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:42.780
Donald Trump with someone and Joe Biden, who
would be no progressive hero, but would certainly

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:48.540
bring back diplomacy and bring back some stability
and bring back some sanity.

